<td id="S4.T2.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.5600</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Ablation on Search Strategies</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further compare search algorithms under the UCB framework. Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3 Ablation on Search Strategies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MAPGD: Multi-Agent Prompt Gradient Descent for Collaborative Prompt Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows that beam search achieves a significantly higher F1 (0.6844) than Monte Carlo sampling (0.50). This suggests that structured search is more effective than purely stochastic exploration in identifying high-quality prompts.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of search algorithms under UCB (test=150, train=50).</span></figcaption>
